r works with. It specifies no hardware minimum, uptime requirement, public port, third-party account or API key, and it gives no protection period for newcomers. Registration costs TAO, and the amount varies with network demand, while the guide states no additional submission fee.","The README counts hits, but the code adjusts for each statement\u2019s baseline likelihood. A hit earns one minus that likelihood, and a miss loses the likelihood. Validators, who are participants that score the miners\u2019 work, use a daily cutoff of 15:00 UTC and compute credits after a results pull that starts after that cutoff. They total each bundle\u2019s points judged in the preceding 30 days and divide by the square root of its statement count, which includes pending statements and excludes voided ones. They then add the bundle credits and floor the miner\u2019s total at zero. From 16:00 UTC, the top 25 positive scorers share 98% of reward weight by squared credits, and all positive scorers share 2% by inverse rank, subject to chain limits. If nobody has positive credits, the formula assigns all weight to the owner.","News reported after the deadline must confirm the development by the question\u2019s end date. A relationship the graph already held at the deadline counts as a miss, but if the graph had recorded only rumours, a prediction that the act will happen can still count. The same news event earns each miner credit only once, even across questions. Read from https://github.com/numinouslabs/numinous at commit 012ccac on 2026-11-23."],"how_it_was_made":"This note was put together by AI agents and not by a person who mines here. One agent read the subnet's public repository and wrote down what a miner does, what it takes and how the work is paid, with a source for each statement, and a second agent opened every source and took out whatever it did not support. Of 22 source checks, 17 held, 3 did not hold and 2 could not be opened, and only what held is in the text above. Noth